---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { oracleWaitlist } from "../data/elsewhere";

const how = [
  { step: "It rebuilds your past work.", detail: "From the sessions Archive keeps, and your tickets, threads and pull requests in Linear, Slack and GitHub, reached through Kingdom: what was asked, what the agent did, where it went wrong, and what you corrected." },
  { step: "It turns that into tests.", detail: "Each test hands the agent a real task from before the work landed. What you merged is the answer key." },
  { step: "It tries changes against them.", detail: "Changes to your docs and to what each Warden knows. Oracle keeps the ones that pass and drops the rest." },
  { step: "It holds some work back.", detail: "About one piece of work in four is set aside and never tuned on, so Oracle can tell a real gain from one that only fits its own tests." },
];
const status = [
  "Running on inixiative's own projects.",
  "Built: linking each piece of finished work to its ticket, pull request, reviews and the agent sessions behind it, and setting aside the held-back share. Reading Slack comes later.",
  "Oracle is closed source.",
];
---
<Base
  title="Oracle: your agents stop making the same mistake · inixiative"
  description="Oracle figures out how to make your coding agent better on your project, from your own past work, and checks that it worked. Join the waitlist."
  domain="agentic"
  current="oracle"
>
  <section class="hero">
    <span class="eyebrow"><span class="status-dot" data-live></span>Oracle</span>
    <h1 class="display-1">Your agents stop making the same mistake.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Oracle figures out how to make your agent better on your project, and checks that it worked.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button" href={oracleWaitlist}>Join the Oracle waitlist</a>
      <a class="button button-secondary" href="/foundry/">Use Foundry today</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow" data-quiet>The problem</span>
      <h2>Told to use what your project has, the agent uses it. Left alone, it builds its own.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Given clear direction, the agent does the work right. It just doesn't remember the direction, so you
        give it again every session.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow" data-quiet>Tested from both sides</span>
      <h2>Does the right reminder fix the work, and can Wardens tell when it's needed?</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="split">
      <div class="text">
        <h3>Given the reminder, the agent plans the right thing.</h3>
        <p>
          The reminder starts as a correction Aron gave. On tasks from our template project, given his
          correction with its reason before it planned, the agent produced the right fix 4 of 4 times.
        </p>
        <p>
          A bare hint, such as the name of the right file or a nudge to search first, made two of those cases
          worse. The reason has to come with the reminder.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="text">
        <h3>Wardens catch most of the moments that need one.</h3>
        <p>
          Wardens are small agents in Foundry, each holding what one part of your project already has. In the
          latest full test, on 2 November 2026, they caught 82% of the moments that called for a reminder
          (93 of 114), and spoke up wrongly on 2% of the rest (29 of 1,350).
        </p>
        <p>
          These numbers are early. They come from our template project, on the same tasks the Wardens were
          tuned on.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow" data-quiet>How Oracle does it</span>
      <h2>Oracle learns from the work you have already done.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Tests that run a full agent are expensive, up to a million tokens for a single plan, so Oracle chooses
        which to run, with cheap checks first and full runs only where they decide something.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ol class="steps">
      {how.map((s) => <li><strong>{s.step}</strong> {s.detail}</li>)}
    </ol>
    <div class="text">
      <h3>Where it stands</h3>
      <ul>{status.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}</ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="text">
        <span class="eyebrow" data-quiet>Why not a skill</span>
        <h2 class="display-3">A skill adds to your agent's context, and context runs out.</h2>
        <p>
          Writing your project's lessons into skills works while there are few of them. Each one adds to what
          your agent carries, and the more it carries, the less of it the agent uses well.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="text">
        <p>
          Foundry splits what your project knows across Wardens, each holding one part, and only what applies
          to the moment reaches your agent. Each Warden stays small: in Foundry's tests, a Warden holding about
          20,000 tokens caught 90% of what it should, and one holding 80,000 caught 67%.
        </p>
        <p>
          Oracle tunes that split: what each Warden holds, and where one part ends and the next begins.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="card feature" data-raised>
      <div class="text">
        <span class="eyebrow"><span class="status-dot"></span>Foundry</span>
        <h2 class="display-3">How Oracle's changes reach your agent today.</h2>
        <p>
          Foundry is the workspace around Claude Code and Codex, free to use and source-available. Its Wardens
          remind your agent of what your project already has and where it's written down, on every turn. When
          Oracle changes a doc or a Warden, Foundry carries that change into your next session.
        </p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button button-secondary" href="/foundry/">How Foundry works</a>
          <a class="text-link" href="/foundry/demo/">Try the demo</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="waitlist">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow" data-quiet>Waitlist</span>
      <h2>Run Oracle on your project.</h2>
      <p class="lede">Join the waitlist to hear when Oracle opens to other projects, with the results as they come in.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button" href={oracleWaitlist}>Join the Oracle waitlist</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
